  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "if you XOR the entire array, the paired numbers cancel out - what are you left with?",
    "you're left with a XOR b, where a and b are the two single numbers - since a != b, this XOR is nonzero, meaning at least one bit differs between them",
    "pick any bit that's set in (a XOR b) - for example the lowest set bit, via (axorb) & (-axorb) - this bit is 1 in exactly one of a or b",
    "split the entire array into two groups based on whether each number has that bit set, and XOR each group independently - one group's XOR is a, the other's is b, then sort the pair before returning"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "trying to XOR the whole array and returning a single value, forgetting the problem wants two distinct numbers",
    "picking a bit position to split on that isn't actually set in (a XOR b), which breaks the separation",
    "forgetting to sort the two results, so the returned order doesn't match the expected deterministic ascending order"
  ],
